For every interval state, $c(I,q)\ge |z(I,q)|$. Both $c(I,\cdot)$ and $|z(I,\cdot)|$ are $1$-Lipschitz in the endpoint metric. If $I$ is partitioned, in order, into $I_1,\ldots,I_m$, and $\alpha=(s_1,\ldots,s_m)$ is any list of valid child states, define $$\operatorname{conn}_r(\alpha)=|s_1^--r^-|
   +\sum_{i=1}^{m-1}|s_{i+1}^--s_i^+|+|s_m^+-r^+|.$$ Then $$\begin{aligned}
 c(I,r)&\le\sum_i c(I_i,s_i)+\operatorname{conn}_r(\alpha),\label{eq:connection}\\
 |z(I,r)|&\le\sum_i|z(I_i,s_i)|+\operatorname{conn}_r(\alpha).
 \label{eq:shift-connection}
\end{aligned}$$ The connection inequalities also hold for finite target intervals in any extension of $y$ beyond its stored endpoints.

Except on an event of probability less than $0.01$, simultaneously for all initial entries, all guesses, all their nodes, and all shifts, $$\label{eq:coarse-accuracy}
 T_w(v)/f_w-a_w\le\widetilde T_w(v)\le f_wT_w(v)+a_w,
 \qquad
 f_w=\left(\frac{1+\lambda}{1-\lambda}\right)^{\operatorname{height}(w)}
 \le2.$$

Use the parameters and interval tree of Section <a href="#sec:geometry" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="sec:geometry">2</a> in the large-input branch. Let $A$ be a power of two with $1\le A\le A_{\rm init}$, and let $U$ be an integer table on every labeled tree node and every valid target state, with $$0\le U(I,q)\le N,\qquad c(I,q)\le U(I,q)\le A c(I,q).$$ Set $a=\max\{1,A/F\}$. There is a randomized, finitely executable refinement table $d_S$ such that, for every state $(I,q)$, $$\label{eq:refinement-guarantee}
 d_S(I,q)\ge c(I,q)/a,\qquad
 \mathbb E\bigl[(d_S(I,q)-c(I,q))_+\bigr]\le c(I,q)/\ell.$$ The first inequality holds for every outcome of the refinement randomness; the expectation in the second is conditional on the fixed table $U$. At zero-cost states the returned value is zero for every outcome.

For the fixed seed in Theorem <a href="#thm:refinement" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:refinement">6</a>, $$\label{eq:initial-cones}
 \max\{c/a,|z|\}\le w_0\le2Fc,
 \qquad \operatorname{Lip}(w_0(I,\cdot))\le L_0.$$ At a nonleaf, the following local rule computes this global minimum exactly. If $U(I,q)=0$, return zero. Otherwise omit the zero cones, enumerate scales $$\label{eq:initial-local-scales}
 U(I,q)/(16A^2)\le b\le4A U(I,q),$$ and enumerate grid centers at sup distance at most $U(I,q)$ from $q$, retaining the eligibility test $b/2\le U(I,r)\le4Ab$.

For a seed satisfying Theorem <a href="#thm:refinement" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:refinement">6</a>, every outcome of the refinement obeys $$\begin{aligned}
 \max\{c/a,|z|\}&\le d_t\le d_{t-1}\le w_0,
 \label{eq:refinement-invariants}\\
 d_t(I,q)&\le(1+\delta)d_t(I,q')+L|q-q'|_1.
 \label{eq:refinement-lipschitz}
\end{aligned}$$ At nonleaves one also has $$\label{eq:bellman-sandwich}
 \mathcal B_I(d_t)(q)\le d_t(I,q)
           \le\mathcal B_I(d_{t-1})(q).$$ For $d_0$, the spatial bound holds with Lipschitz constant $L$ and without the factor $1+\delta$.

Under the seed hypothesis of Theorem <a href="#thm:refinement" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:refinement">6</a>, fix complete child tables and one group at time $t$. With conditional probability at least $1-H^{-4}$, all action-cost estimates used to choose its representative are within $\gamma b$ of their actual costs. Consequently, for any specified unskipped member $(b,r)$ of this group, on the same event, simultaneously for every valid parent state $q$ and every $\alpha\in\mathcal C_{b,r}$, $$\label{eq:group-accuracy}
 \Pi_t(I,q)\le L|q-r|_1+(1+\delta)x_t(\alpha)+e_*b,
 \qquad e_*=2\gamma+512LF\theta\le\kappa\delta.$$ Here the inequality uses the global cone minimum, equivalently its local evaluation from Lemma <a href="#lem:cone-locality" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:cone-locality">8</a>.

At each nonterminal of the comparison tree, $(b,r)$ is a representative, $b$ satisfies the local scale restriction <a href="#eq:local-scales" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:local-scales">[eq:local-scales]</a>, and $\alpha^*$ is a wide action whose endpoint shifts are within $6b$ of $p_0=r^- -l$. Writing $c_i=c(I_i,q_i)$, we have $$\label{eq:comparison-split}
 c(I,r)\le\sum_i c_i\le (1+4M/P)c,
 \qquad c\le Le+\sum_i c_i.$$ Let $C_{\mathrm{term}}$ be the sum of terminal true costs. Then $$\label{eq:comparison-totals}
 \begin{aligned}
 C_{\mathrm{term}}&\le(1+8MJ/P)D\le2D,\\
 \sum_{\mathrm{nonterminal}}c&\le2JD,
 &\sum_{\mathrm{nonterminal}}e&\le4JD/P.
 \end{aligned}$$ The sum of true costs at any depth is at most $2D$, also if stopped costs are carried unchanged to subsequent depths. At every terminal, $m=c$ and every refinement estimate equals $c$.

On the fixed comparison tree, put $h=Le+w_0(I,q)/T$ at each nonterminal. Then $$\label{eq:curvature-structural}
 \sum_{\mathrm{nonterminal}}(K+G)
 \le4C_{\mathrm{term}}+3\sum_{\mathrm{nonterminal}}e
                       +4\sum_{\mathrm{nonterminal}}h.$$ Consequently, $$\label{eq:curvature-total}
 \sum_{\mathrm{nonterminal}}(K+G)
 \le5000F(1+J/P+J/T)D.$$

The total true cost of bad nodes satisfies $$\label{eq:bad-node-cost}
 \sum_{\mathrm{bad}}c
 \le10000FH^{1/4}(1+J/P+J/T)D.$$

Condition on the complete child tables at such a comparison node. Then $$\label{eq:node-regret}
\begin{split}
 \mathbb E\left[\sum_{t=1}^S\mathcal R_t
       \,\middle|\,\text{child tables}\right]
 \le {}&h_\Sigma\left(
       \frac{\ln d_{\mathcal C}}{\eta}
       +2\kappa+SH^{-3}\right)\\
       &+2\delta c+2SH^{-4}Fc.
\end{split}$$ Here $h_\Sigma\le64Fb$ and $\ln d_{\mathcal C}\le4(p_*+21)\ell$.
